        "id": 11059
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-08-30T03:07:32Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It feels like this violates the abstraction between the two classes; we\u0027re incrementing the error count in one place and then undoing the increment in another totally different class. \n\nAlternate idea: what if `handleError` returns a value; returning 1 (or true?) means \"I reported it\" and 0 (or false?) means \"I decided not to report this error\". So you could do\n\n    fErrorCount +\u003d handleError(msg, pos);\n\nor\n\n    if (handleError(msg, pos)) { ++fErrorCount; }",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 995,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 995,
        "endChar": 78
      },
      "revId": "db7ce41db9418547d024f1c0b059ccb533916d5f",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "457de042_459be860",
        "filename": "src/sksl/SkSLCompiler.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 995,
      "author": {
        "id": 5640
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-08-30T21:14:16Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I had considered that approach, but didn\u0027t like it either given that this is ostensibly a public API. Not only do I think it would be unclear to people why they might need to ignore some errors, letting them ignore arbitrary errors could potentially result in some weird and possibly even dangerous behaviors as the compiler follows untested code paths with broken code.\n\nI think maybe the best way to address this is to move the poison check higher up so that it never even makes it into handleError. Tell me what you think.",
      "parentUuid": "0d413362_4c9c43c7",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 995,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 995,
        "endChar": 78
      },
      "revId": "db7ce41db9418547d024f1c0b059ccb533916d5f",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
